<header><p class="st-kicker">theocracy · China, Tibet and Mongolia</p><h1 class="st-title">Tibet (1912-1951)</h1><p class="st-meta">1912 CE – 1951 CE · capital Lhasa</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Tibet under the Dalai Lamas' government in its decades of de facto independence, between the fall of the Qing and the arrival of the People's Liberation Army.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/qing-empire">Qing Empire</a> collapsed, Tibetan forces expelled the Chinese garrison from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lhasa">Lhasa</a> in 1912, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thubten-gyatso">Thirteenth Dalai Lama</a> returned from exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> in early 1913, issuing a proclamation that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibetans">Tibetans</a> have since read as a declaration of independence. The government of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/ganden-phodrang">Ganden Phodrang</a> continued in Lhasa.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>At the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/simla-convention">Simla Convention</a> of 1914 <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-kingdom">Britain</a> and Tibet agreed a border (the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mcmahon-line">McMahon Line</a>), but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> refused to ratify the convention. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thubten-gyatso">Thirteenth Dalai Lama</a> built a modern army, introduced stamps, currency and a short-lived English school, but powerful monasteries resisted reform. After his death in 1933 regents such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reting-rinpoche">Reting Rinpoche</a> ruled, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/14th-dalai-lama">Fourteenth Dalai Lama</a> was enthroned in 1940.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In October 1950 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peoples-liberation-army">People's Liberation Army</a> defeated the Tibetan army at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-chamdo">Chamdo</a>. In May 1951 Tibetan delegates in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a> signed the Seventeen Point Agreement, and Chinese troops entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lhasa">Lhasa</a> that autumn. Tibet became part of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/peoples-republic-of-china">People's Republic of China</a>, though its status remains disputed.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/tibet-1912-1951.jpg" alt="Tibet (1912-1951)" width="320" height="226"><figcaption>Image: University of Texas Libraries · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tibet-claims.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1912 CE – 1951 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>theocracy</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>theocratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Lhasa</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Thirteenth Dalai Lama, Reting Rinpoche (regent), Fourteenth Dalai Lama</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Tibetan Buddhism (Gelug)</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Tibetan</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/china">China</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Ganden Phodrang (1912-1951), de facto independent Tibet</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1932&amp;focus=tibet-1912-1951" aria-label="Tibet (1912-1951) on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7051 1094 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Tibet (1912-1951) lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7371 1350L7372 1351M7371 1350L7868 1006"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7372 1351h0M7868 1006h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7371 1350h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7371 1350h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1932&amp;focus=tibet-1912-1951">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
